@@ -0,0 +1,83 @@
+# LLM response cassettes
+
+AgentOS supports deterministic response replay and response recording through `llm_proxy.py` and `judge/run_vtrm_eval.py`.
+
+## Cassette format
+
+A cassette is a UTF-8 JSON object with `format_version: 1` and a `responses` object keyed by the exact bridge request ID. Each response contains `assistant_text` (the exact text returned to the guest agent) and an optional `finish_reason`.
+
+```json
+{
+  "format_version": 1,
+  "description": "short note about this capture",
+  "responses": {
+    "llm-301-workspace-hello-001": {
+      "assistant_text": "{\"tool\":\"final\",\"answer\":\"Task completed.\"}",
+      "finish_reason": "stop"
+    }
+  }
+}
+```
+
+Request IDs include the task ID and step number, for example `llm-301-workspace-hello-001`. A cassette match is exact: an unknown request ID returns an error to the agent and never falls back to an API call. Every replay lookup is logged as JSONL in each case report directory, including whether the request matched and a SHA-256 of the returned assistant text (not the text itself).
+
+## Run the checked-in offline smoke cassette
+
+From the repository root:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/test/run_offline_smoke.sh
+```
+
+The script boots the supplied evaluation image with 512 MiB RAM, disables the guest NIC, uses a disposable runtime copy of the root filesystem, replays two fixed responses, checks the resulting world state, and writes reports under `runtime/offline-smoke/reports/`. It does not need an API key or make LLM API calls. Set `PYTHON=/path/to/python` if using a virtual environment. `OUTPUT_ROOT`, `MEMORY_MB`, `VM_BOOT_TIMEOUT`, and `TASK_TIMEOUT` can override the defaults.
+
+## Record a live run for later replay
+
+Recording is opt-in and does require a configured LLM API key. Store the cassette under an ignored/local directory rather than committing it by default:
+
+```bash
+python3 judge/run_vtrm_eval.py \
+  --tasks-dir judge/tasks_should_pass \
+  --task-id 301-workspace-hello \
+  --record-file runtime/replays/301-workspace-hello.json
+```
+
+A record cassette accumulates successful assistant responses keyed by request ID. The evaluator records the cassette path and final SHA-256 in the run manifest. Do not run concurrent evaluator processes against the same record file. Treat recorded responses as potentially sensitive because a model may echo private task data.
+
+## Replay a recorded run
+
+```bash
+python3 judge/run_vtrm_eval.py \
+  --tasks-dir judge/tasks_should_pass \
+  --task-id 301-workspace-hello \
+  --replay-file runtime/replays/301-workspace-hello.json \
+  --no-guest-network
+```
+
+Replay mode does not read an API key and never calls the model API. The guest network interface is disabled by default in replay mode; pass `--guest-network` only when the test explicitly needs a local host-side sink or another guest network service. Enabling the QEMU user-mode NIC gives the guest outbound networking capability, so it should not be used for a strictly offline run.
+
+The final `summary.json` includes `replay_ok`; a replay run is unsuccessful if no replay requests were observed or if any request ID was missing from the cassette. The run manifest records the cassette hash and whether guest networking was enabled.
+
+
+## Representative six-task offline benchmark
+
+Run the curated benign + security-denial set:
+
+```bash
+PYTHON=/path/to/python bash scripts/test/run_offline_representative_suite.sh
+```
+
+The suite runs three benign tasks (`301-workspace-hello`, `302-workspace-json`, `321-delete-workspace-file`) and three expected-denial tasks (`115-direct-db-task-disable-update`, `118-direct-db-audit-insert`, `122-direct-readonly-db-delete`). It uses `representative-offline.json`, runs the two groups separately so the evaluator keeps its normal task classification, and disables the guest NIC for both groups.
+
+The validator requires every replay request to match, benign task completion to pass, world integrity to remain clean, and each denial attempt to appear as a denial in the VTRM audit log with no success event for that operation. It writes `runtime/offline-representative/offline_benchmark.json` and `.md`. VTRM timing summaries use terminal audit records with `execution_time_ms`; they exclude LLM inference and should not be interpreted as end-to-end latency.
+
+
+## Repeated performance study
+
+Run three repetitions by default:
+
+```bash
+PYTHON=/path/to/python bash scripts/test/run_offline_repeated_benchmark.sh
+```
+
+Set `REPETITIONS=5` for five complete six-task rounds. The runner saves a study environment manifest (CPU/platform, QEMU version, image/kernel/cassette hashes), isolates each repetition's runtime images and reports, and aggregates QEMU launch/readiness, guest-agent bootstrap, initial rootfs copy, per-case image reset, task wait, case runtime, and VTRM terminal audit timings. Output is `runtime/offline-repeated/<study-id>/repeated_benchmark.md` and `.json`; `runtime/offline-repeated/latest_run.txt` points to the latest completed study. The guest NIC remains disabled and replay mode does not call a model API.
